= Staal Concedes

Staal, having willfully avoided any serious effort to preserve his chancellorship—for reasons known only to himself—has now resigned himself to the inevitable loss of a position he never truly desired.

In what is likely his final cabinet meeting, he informs his ministers that he will request a decree to dissolve the Assembly, as a no-confidence vote is now inevitable. He fully expects his request to be denied and is prepared to submit both his and the cabinet's resignation.

In his final days, he turns his focus to opposing a Goodryke restoration, even going so far as to express a preference for a [+ royalist_chancellor +] government—believing, perhaps naively, that he might retain his post as Defense Minister under such an arrangement.

- @schleicher_2: Staal meets with President DuFour, perhaps for the last time.

@schleicher_2

Staal lays out four scenarios before the President:

1. A [+ royalist_chancellor +] cabinet supported by the Assembly—unlikely.

2. A presidential cabinet led by [+ royalist_chancellor +]—even more implausible.

3. His own cabinet continuing under presidential emergency powers—his recommendation.

4. A disastrous cabinet led by Goodryke and Mallin—likely to provoke civil war.

He does not request a postponement of elections this time—only a commitment from DuFour to dissolve the chamber upon reconvening.

He is denied. DuFour acknowledges Staal’s efforts to win over the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>, but with that effort having failed, the President declares it time to explore “another possibility.”

- @schleicher_3

@schleicher_3
title: Staal resigns.

Staal returns to his waiting ministers and relays the outcome. An announcement of his resignation—prepared weeks in advance—is released to the press.

At this stage, the options have narrowed to two: a return of Goodryke, or the ascension of [+ royalist_chancellor +]. Both promise disaster for the Commonwealth—and the latter, disaster for all of humanity.

- @schleicher_4

@schleicher_4
title: The cabinet's last order of business.
on-arrival: disfavored -= 2; economic_growth += 1; inflation += 2; staal_approval += 1

After weeks of delay, Aveling finally presents his public works program to the cabinet. Though too late to save Staal’s tenure, it is approved by the chancellor and signed by DuFour without objection. The plan allocates half a billion crowns to infrastructure projects across the Commonwealth.

In the coming months, as millions of unemployed Germans return to work, it is not Staal, but his successor, who will reap the political rewards.